| **1. Submit your resignation** | Write a formal resignation letter (email is acceptable) stating your intended last working day. | Your manager → HR | At least **30 days** before your last day (15 days if you are still on probation). |
| **2. Notice period** | Serve the required notice (30 days / 15 days during probation). The company may waive this notice at its discretion. | You & your manager | During the notice period you’ll continue to work as usual. |
| **3. Clearance & hand‑over** | Complete the clearance checklist covering: <br>• IT (return laptops, access cards, revoke system access) <br>• Finance (settle any advances, submit expense reimbursements) <br>• HR (hand over pending tasks, update employee records) | IT, Finance, HR | Should be finished **before** your last working day. |
| **4. Final settlement** | After clearance, the company processes your full and final settlement, which includes any pending reimbursements, leave encashment, and statutory dues. | Finance & HR | Settlement is completed **within 45 days** of your last working day. |
| **5. Exit interview (optional)** | You may be invited for an exit interview to share feedback. | HR | Usually scheduled during the last week. |

### Key Points to Remember
- **Notice period:** 30 days (15 days if you are still on probation). The company can waive this notice, but you should still confirm the final last working day in writing.
- **Clearance:** All three departments (IT, Finance, HR) must sign off before you leave. Failure to complete clearance can delay your final settlement.
- **Final payout:** Full and final settlement, including any leave encashment or reimbursements, will be processed **within 45 days** after your last day.

